# Harmonic Spectral Witnesses

The flow calculus page introduces the Lawvere Laplacian as closure motion. This
page teaches the spectral language that is lawful at the same phase. "Kernel",
"H0", "Rayleigh quotient", and "spectral gap" do not enter as Hilbert-space
primitives. They enter when a closure operator has generated fixed points,
global sections, finite tile energies, and explicit polynomial witnesses.

The claim-site carried here is:

* claim-site: harmonic and spectral witnesses generated by closure flow;
* phase: closure phase, after modal closure and before physical wave or plasma
  pages borrow spectral language;
* generating sites: Lawvere Laplacian fixed points, closure image as global
  sections, algebraic Laplacianizer, rational tile Dirichlet energy, finite
  path gaps, and integer polynomial brackets;
* downstream uses: closure-flow singularity pages, causal wave and spacetime
  operators, plasma wave projections, chromatic action pages, and frontier spectral
  diagnostics;
* Lean declarations: `Laplacianizer`, `settlingLaplacianizer`,
  `settling_is_laplacianizable`, `HarmonicKernel`, `H0Object`,
  `harmonic_kernel_h0_iso`, `dirichlet`, `rayleigh_ge_bottom`,
  `harmonic_iff_constant`, `chain_index_balance`, `tile_gap_char_root`,
  `tile7_gap_bracket`, `properK4Colourings_count`, `k4_four_analytic_spine`,
  `orthogonal_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained`,
  `triangular_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained`,
  `king_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained`,
  `corpus_planar_tile_patches_four_colorable`,
  `tile5_first_noncrystallographic_quadratic_surd`, `doubledPhi`, `doubledGap5`,
  `doubledPhi_add_doubledGap5`, `RReal.inv`, `RReal.mul_inv`, `half`, `phi`, `gap5`,
  `phi_sq`, `phi_add_gap5`;
* Blueprint/proof-tree nodes: the Lawvere-Laplacian and settling-cohomology
  nodes supply the intended generator; no Blueprint node yet promotes general
  analytic spectral theory;
* provenance: Lawvere-Laplacian Grothendieck-site notes, laplacianizer notes,
  chromatic-Lorentzian tile notes, and anti-Landauer H0 witnesses;
* familiar mathematical comparison: Hodge isomorphism, harmonic kernel, Rayleigh
  quotient, positive semidefinite Laplacian, Poincare gap, quadratic surd, and
  cubic spectral root;
* reader closure: identify the finite closure, H0, or tile-energy carrier before
  reading any analytic spectral term;
* frontier status: inner products over arbitrary finite-dimensional real vector
  spaces, self-adjoint spectral theorem, complex roots, continuum wave spectra,
  and general Perron-Frobenius/Hodge theory remain missing carriers.

The governing sentence is:

> Spectral language is lawful here only as the spectrum of generated closure
> witnesses.

## Laplacianizable Settling

The algebraic laplacianizer packages the data that a closure site needs before
"Hodge" language can be spoken: a Laplacian, monotonicity, harmonic states, and
an isomorphism between cohomology and the harmonic kernel.

The carrier is
{name Obsmetrologia.Laplacianizers.Laplacianizer}`Laplacianizer`: a bundle of
objects, chains, an order Laplacian, cohomology, and Hodge maps.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Laplacianizers.Laplacianizer.Harmonic}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Laplacianizers.Laplacianizer.hodge_isomorphism}

The settling site supplies this data without importing Hilbert geometry:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Laplacianizers.settlingLaplacianizer}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Laplacianizers.settling_is_laplacianizable}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Laplacianizers.settling_hodge_isomorphism}

Checked:

```lean
example (M : ModalFrame) :
    Laplacianizable Unit
      (fun _ => settledSections M) :=
  settling_is_laplacianizable M

example (M : ModalFrame) :
    (∀ v : Valuation,
      IsHarmonic M v ↔ M.closureOp v = v) :=
  (settling_hodge_isomorphism M).2.2
```

This is the phase boundary. The Hodge projection is allowed because the maps and
round trips are in Lean. Continuous inner-product moduli are not.

## Harmonic Kernel As H0

The next promotion separates two presentations that are not definitionally the
same: fixed points of the Lawvere Laplacian and global sections as the image of
closure. Idempotence proves they coincide.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.LawvereLaplacian.HarmonicKernel}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.LawvereLaplacian.H0Object}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.LawvereLaplacian.mem_h0_iff_harmonic}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.LawvereLaplacian.harmonic_kernel_h0_iso}

Checked:

```lean
example (M : ModalFrame) :
    (∀ v : Valuation,
      (∃ u : Valuation, M.closureOp u = v)
        ↔ IsHarmonic M v) :=
  (harmonic_kernel_h0_iso M).1
```

The reader closure is precise: H0 here is not a general sheaf-cohomology engine.
It is the closure image/global-section carrier for this generated Laplacian.

## Rational Tile Rayleigh Witness

The chromatic tile supplies a finite rational inner-product layer. The
Dirichlet energy is a sum of squares of consecutive differences, so positivity
and the Rayleigh bottom are dependency-free.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.normSq}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.diffs}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.dirichlet}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.Flat}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.rayleigh_ge_bottom}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.harmonic_iff_constant}

Checked:

```lean
example (v : List Real.Internal.Rat) :
    (0 : Real.Internal.Rat) * normSq v
      ≤ dirichlet v :=
  rayleigh_ge_bottom v

example (v : List Real.Internal.Rat) :
    dirichlet v = 0 ↔ IsConst v :=
  harmonic_iff_constant v
```

The theorem is spectral only in this finite sense: bottom eigenvalue zero,
kernel equals constants, and positivity comes from rational sums of squares.

## Realized Tile-Adjacency Chromatics

The chromatic tile carrier also has a finite adjacency side. The corpus uses
three realized convention profiles here: orthogonal, triangular, and king
adjacency. The Lean theorem does not invoke a general planar four-colour theorem.
It proves the finite profile directly:

* orthogonal adjacency is the two-phase complete obstruction;
* triangular adjacency is the three-phase complete obstruction;
* king adjacency is the FOUR complete obstruction.

The exact witnesses are:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics.orthogonal_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics.triangular_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics.king_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained}

The finite corpus quantifier is named:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics.CorpusTileAdjacencyConvention}

and the no-more-than-FOUR theorem is:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics.corpus_planar_tile_patches_four_colorable}

Checked:

```lean
namespace Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics

example :
    Colorable orthogonalAdjacency OrthogonalPhase
      ∧ ¬ Colorable orthogonalAdjacency PUnit :=
  orthogonal_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained

example :
    Colorable triangularAdjacency TriangularPhase
      ∧ ¬ Colorable triangularAdjacency OrthogonalPhase :=
  triangular_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained

example :
    Colorable kingAdjacency Charge
      ∧ ¬ Colorable kingAdjacency TriangularPhase :=
  king_chromaticNumber_bounded_attained

example (r : CorpusTileAdjacencyConvention) :
    Colorable
      (CorpusTileAdjacencyConvention.graph r)
      Charge :=
  corpus_planar_tile_patches_four_colorable r

end Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics
```

This is the scope boundary. The theorem says every corpus patch represented by
those realized finite carriers is FOUR-colourable. It does not claim arbitrary
planar graph four-colourability, and it does not use a computer-assisted search.

The same complete FOUR obstruction has a finite information count:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics.properK4Colourings_count}

Checked:

```lean
example :
    Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics.properK4Colourings.length = 24 :=
  Obsmetrologia.Combinatorics.properK4Colourings_count
```

## H0 Index Balance

The same finite chain has a one-dimensional harmonic kernel. The anti-Landauer
side records this as H0, with no irreversible Landauer bits on the chain.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.chainNerve}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.chain_index_balance}

Checked:

```lean
example (n : Nat) :
    antiLandauerBits (chainNerve n) = 1
      ∧ Obsmetrologia.Landauer.landauerBits
        (chainNerve n) = 0 :=
  ⟨(chain_index_balance n).1,
    (chain_index_balance n).2.1⟩
```

This is where the harmonic H0 sentence is established for later physical pages
to reuse. It is a finite index witness, not an analytic index theorem.

## Small Gap Certificates

The smallest path tiles have exact rational gap witnesses. The two-site gap is
exact over rationals; the three-site certificate is carried over the integer
sublattice with a sum-of-squares identity.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.twoSite_gap}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.twoSite_eigenvector}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.iThreeSite_gap_lower}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.ChromaticTileRayleigh.iThreeSite_eigenvector}

Checked:

```lean
example :
    dirichlet [(1 : Real.Internal.Rat), -1]
      = normSq [(1 : Real.Internal.Rat), -1]
        + normSq [(1 : Real.Internal.Rat), -1] :=
  twoSite_eigenvector

example :
    idirichlet [(1 : Int), 0, -1]
      = inormSq [(1 : Int), 0, -1] :=
  iThreeSite_eigenvector
```

The promotion rule is simple: a named gap is settled only when its finite
Rayleigh witness or polynomial witness is present. Otherwise it is a projection.

## K4/FOUR Analytic Spine

The K4/FOUR carrier and the analytic chromatic carrier now meet in one Lean
theorem. The statement is not a new continuum theorem. It is a bundle saying
that the four-element K4 action, the exact FOUR chromatic obstruction, the
`4! = 24` K4 colouring count, the Rayleigh floor, the small gap witnesses, the
quadratic and cubic gap certificates, and the finite Fourier-side phase/mass
readout are all present at the same finite claim-site.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.k4_four_analytic_spine}

Checked:

```lean
#check Obsmetrologia.Flow.k4_four_analytic_spine
```

This is the sense in which the analytic side is chaseable from the other
direction: K4 forces the finite palette from the chromatic side, while
Rayleigh, gap, and finite diffraction witnesses read analytic structure back
off the finite carrier. The general spectral formula, the constructed cubic
root, and infinite diffraction remain frontier promotions.

## Quadratic And Cubic Frontiers

The four-, five-, and six-site gaps are represented by quadratic surd root
witnesses in the internal reals. The seven-site gap is the first cubic frontier:
the module records integer polynomial reduction and sign brackets, but does not
yet construct the full real root by bisection.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile_gap_char_root}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile4_gap_char_root}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile5_gap_char_root}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile6_gap_char_root}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile7GapCubic}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile7_gap_bracket}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.cos7_bracket}

Checked:

```lean
example :
    (Obsmetrologia.Flow.evalIntPoly
      Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile7GapCubic 0 < 0)
      ∧ 0 < Obsmetrologia.Flow.evalIntPoly
        Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile7GapCubic 1 :=
  Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile7_gap_bracket
```

This is honest frontier language. Quadratic gaps have real surd carriers here.
The cubic seven-site gap has a bracket and reduction, not yet a constructed
`RReal` root. A page that needs that root must build the bisection-IVT carrier.

## The Pentagon Gap Is Not Crystallographic, And Not A New Field

The five-site gap yields two more finite claims once it is placed next to its
neighbours. First: the crystallographic family (`a = 2`, the orders that give
the two-, three-, four-, and six-site tiles their gaps) needs `d ≤ 4`, and
`d = 5` breaks that bound outright.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile5_first_noncrystallographic_quadratic_surd}

Checked:

```lean
example :
    ¬ (5 ≤ 2 * 2) :=
  Obsmetrologia.Flow.tile5_first_noncrystallographic_quadratic_surd.1
```

Second: the same gap is not a new surd. It is the golden mean's own field
element, read at a different normalization. `MetallicRoot`'s `n = 1` case
already carries the golden mean, division-free, as `1 + √5`; doubled here as
`doubledPhi`. Doubled the same way, the pentagon gap is `doubledGap5`. The two
are complementary to `4`:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.doubledPhi}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.doubledGap5}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.doubledPhi_add_doubledGap5}

`Calculus.Real.Inverse` closes the gap the doubled form left open: genuine
`RReal` division on the positive-apart-from-zero fragment, Bishop-style (an
explicit rational lower bound witnesses invertibility — no `Classical.choice`).
`half := 1/2` is built from it, and `phi`/`gap5` are the actual, undoubled `φ`
and `λ₅`:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.half}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.two_mul_half}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.phi}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.gap5}

Undoubling is not just cosmetic — it makes the golden ratio's own defining
equation statable, which the doubled form could only phrase as `(2φ)² =
2·(2φ)+4`:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.phi_sq}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.AperiodicOrder.Tiling.StepN.phi_add_gap5}

Checked:

```lean
example :
    phi * phi = phi + RReal.ofRat (Rat.ofNat 1) :=
  phi_sq

example :
    phi + gap5 = RReal.ofRat (Rat.ofNat 2) :=
  phi_add_gap5
```

The reader-facing content is now exact and undoubled: `√5`, `φ`, `2 - φ`, and
`(3-√5)/2` name one field element, not four coincidentally similar surds. This
is the spectral leg of the Blueprint's Dervish Recurrence Conjecture; the
algebraic-surface and quasicrystal-geometry legs of that conjecture remain
frontier. General nonzero-real inverse (beyond the positive fragment) is not
built — every real inverted so far is manifestly positive, so that is the
honest scope; a general version is a mechanical follow-up.

## Reader Closure

When later books invoke spectra, waves, gaps, kernels, or Hodge language, check
which carrier is doing the work:

* closure fixed point or closure image for harmonic H0;
* rational list energy for finite Rayleigh claims;
* integer sum-of-squares identity for small gap lower bounds;
* internal-real quadratic root for surd gaps;
* integer sign bracket for cubic-gap frontier claims;
* doubled-normalization complementarity for cross-gap field identities (no
  real division carrier exists yet).

Anything stronger remains a promotion task, not a theorem borrowed from a
familiar analytic invariant.
